 # A profile is evidence: what the sampler saw, and what it means
 
-<!-- TODO: one paragraph, in this project's voice, on the difference between a counter and a
-sampler: a counter reports the work somebody wrote down, and a sampler reports where the process
-actually was. Say which of the two the block at the end of this page is, and why the two are read
-together rather than one instead of the other. -->
+A counter and a sampler answer two different questions, and this page reads both.
 
-[TODO](TODO)
+A counter says *what work the run counted*. [The phase profile](perf.md) made that a value with
+seven phases, and `lakelet-load --profile` prints it for a load loop of its own: no clock reaches
+those numbers, so two runs of the same workload print the same bytes, and the block further down
+this page is one of them quoted so that it can be re-run rather than believed.
+
+A sampler says *where the process actually was*. It is the only instrument in this project that can
+disagree with the counters, and when the two disagree the disagreement is the finding rather than a
+gap in either one. This page is one reading of both, and the finding below is exactly that kind.
 
 ## What was sampled, and with what
 
-<!-- TODO: run `kiln doctor` and copy its profilers section — which sampler works on this machine
-is that section's business and not a matter of taste. Then the build and the run: start
-`lakelet-load --profile --seconds 30`, note the pid, and sample that pid with `sample <pid>
-<seconds> -file out.txt`. Say which build you profiled, and why that one. -->
+`lakelet-load --profile` is the mode that puts this process in the state you want to look at: one
+cycle ingests a batch through the protocol path, seals it into a store of its own, and asks the
+store one question about the span it just wrote ([the mode](../src/bin/lakelet-load/profile.rs)).
+`--seconds 30` runs that loop for thirty seconds; what it prints is not the point of that run — the
+*process* is.
+
+Which sampler to use is not a matter of taste, and it is not the same answer on two machines.
+`kiln doctor` answers it by running the tools rather than by looking for them, and here it says:
+
+```text
+profilers
+  ✓ sample (/usr/bin/sample) — `sample <pid> <seconds> -file out.txt` works on your own processes without root
+  ✗ samply — no such command (cargo install samply --locked)
+      needed for a flame graph you can open and share; the platform sampler above is what works here
+  ! cargo flamegraph (…) is installed and cannot run here: it drives `dtrace`, and a `dtrace` this
+    user can start refuses with "DTrace requires additional privileges"
+```
+
+Three lines, three answers, and they are worth the space: `sample` is here and needs no root for a
+process you own; `cargo-flamegraph` is the trap — installed, plausible, and refused, because the
+`dtrace` it drives wants privileges this user does not have; and `samply`, the portable answer, is
+not installed at all. A reader whose `kiln doctor` says something else should believe their own
+doctor; the shape of the reading below does not change.
+
+The build matters too. This is a release build, because the samples of a debug build are largely
+samples of unoptimized code — a fact about `cargo build` rather than about this store:
+
+```sh
+cargo build --release -p lakelet --bin lakelet-load
+./target/release/lakelet-load --profile --seconds 30 &
+sample $! 5 -file /private/tmp/lakelet-load.sample.txt
+```
+
+The loader prints nothing until it stops, so the pid a shell reports for the background job is the
+one to sample — `$!` in the line above, and `pgrep -n lakelet-load` if it is already running.
+
+`sample`'s report is long. Its last section — every leaf frame the sampler landed in, heaviest
+first — is the answer, and this is what it printed here:
+
+```text
+Sort by top of stack, same collapsed (when >= 5):
+        __fcntl  (in libsystem_kernel.dylib)        2180
+        __open  (in libsystem_kernel.dylib)        393
+        __rename  (in libsystem_kernel.dylib)        160
+        write  (in libsystem_kernel.dylib)        119
+        _RNvMCseAmp1tIiz26_9crc32fastNtB2_6Hasher6update  (in lakelet-load)        53
+        _RNvMNtNtNtCs1eoBWF22PJM_7lakelet6engine5index5bloomNtB2_5Bloom6insert  (in lakelet-load)        45
+        _xzm_free  (in libsystem_malloc.dylib)        38
+        _xzm_xzone_malloc  (in libsystem_malloc.dylib)        28
+        close  (in libsystem_kernel.dylib)        23
+        <deduplicated_symbol>  (in libsystem_malloc.dylib)        21
+        ZSTD_decompressSequences  (in lakelet-load)        20
+```
 
 ## What the two symbols at the top are doing
 
-<!-- TODO: copy the last section of `sample`'s report in, and name the two symbols at its top. For
-each: what it is doing in this store, and the call graph above that section is what says where it
-comes from. Then say how much of the report this store's own code got. -->
+**`__fcntl`, 2180 samples — waiting for the device.** The name is the `fcntl(2)` syscall, and the
+call graph above that section says which call it is: `Segments::seal` reaches it four times inside
+one cycle — the segment file, its sidecar index, the pending manifest, and the directory the
+manifest is renamed into — and every one of those is `File::sync_all → fcntl → __fcntl`. `sync_all`
+is the syscall [the write-path record](adr/0001-write-path.md) measured at 4.173 ms, and that
+record shows it *is* `fcntl(fd, F_FULLFSYNC)` on this platform, from the toolchain's source rather
+than from two numbers agreeing. Four of those in one cycle is most of a cycle, and the sampler is
+what says so.
+
+**`__open`, 393 samples — the writes the flush then waits for.** `std::fs::write` opens the file it
+writes and closes it, and a flush opens that file again to sync it, so each of the four writes in a
+cycle contributes an `open(2)` under `write_manifest` and `index::write`. That makes the second
+symbol the second half of the first story rather than a second story: this store is not slow at
+*computing*, it is slow at *committing*.
+
+Then the list gets small: `crc32fast::Hasher::update` is 53 samples and `index::bloom::Bloom::insert`
+45 — the checksum the frame codec computes and the sidecar index a `seal` builds — and the heaviest
+frame from the *read* half of the loop, `ZSTD_decompressSequences`, is 20. What the seven phases
+charge is that read half, and the report says it is the small part. That is the whole of the
+finding: this process spends its time where no phase is charged.
 
 ## The change the numbers justify
 
-<!-- TODO: one sentence naming what to change next, tied to the phase counters — if the top symbol
-is the decoder, say which phase's count it corresponds to. Then say what would have to move in the
-counters for the change to be visible; if nothing would, say so and say why that is the finding
-rather than a gap in the page. -->
+**The durability of a sealed segment should be a policy, the way the durability of an appended
+record already is.** `lakelet/4.3-write-path-adr` measured one durable write at 4.173 ms and turned
+"how often does the store wait for the device" into a value — `wal::Policy`, `interval(50ms)` — for
+the write path it owned. The segment path that `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` and
+`lakelet/4.8-compaction` added never got one: `Segments::seal`, `index::write` and `write_manifest`
+wait for the device unconditionally, four times per sealed segment, and a load loop that seals one
+segment per cycle spends its life inside `fcntl`. The change the numbers justify is to give that
+path the same decision the record path got.
+
+What would have to move in the counters for that change to be visible: **none of the seven.** The
+phases count the *query*; a flush is the write path, and no phase charges it, so the sampler is the
+only instrument here that can see this at all. That is worth stating plainly instead of working
+around: a counter and a profile are not two spellings of one number, they are two instruments, and
+this step has a page because one of them was blind.
+
+The counters are not blind to everything, and this page's block is what they do see. `decode` is
+`records` exactly — a cycle asks about the span it just wrote, so the loop reads precisely what it
+ingested — and the rest follows from that: ten times the batch moves `decode`, `filter` and
+`aggregate` with `records`, while `blocks` and `decompress` stay one per cycle and `headers` stays
+`cycles * (cycles + 1) / 2`. A change to the *workload* is visible in the counters and a change to
+the *flush* is not, and knowing which of the two a number belongs to is what this page is for.
 
 ## Running it yourself
 
-<!-- TODO: `lakelet-load --profile --cycles <n>` for an `<n>` you state below. The block is that
-command's whole output, line for line: one line per phase in the order a query walks them, then
-`cycles=`, `records=` and `bytes=`. Walk the identities a reader can check by hand. -->
+This is the whole output of `lakelet-load --profile --cycles 8`, on a store the run makes for
+itself, and it is the one block here that a reader can compare byte for byte:
 
 ```text
-lakelet-load headers=TODO
+lakelet-load headers=36
+lakelet-load blocks=8
+lakelet-load decompress=8
+lakelet-load decode=8000
+lakelet-load filter=8000
+lakelet-load aggregate=8000
+lakelet-load render=8
+lakelet-load cycles=8
+lakelet-load records=8000
+lakelet-load bytes=232832
 ```
 
+One line per phase, in the order a query walks them and under the names
+[the counter](../src/engine/trace/counters.rs) spells them with, and then the shape of the
+workload. The lines are checkable by hand: `headers` is `36 = 8 * 9 / 2`, one manifest entry for
+every segment that existed in every one of the eight cycles; `blocks` and `decompress` are `8`, one
+segment read per cycle; `decode`, `filter` and `aggregate` are `8000`, the records those cycles
+ingested and every one of them folded into a window; `render` is `8`, one window per cycle; and
+`bytes` is the 232,832 bytes of frames the loader's own workload generator
+([`simulate`](../src/bin/lakelet-load.rs)) produced for them. The mode's own documentation states
+the same identities as invariants of the loop, so a change that broke one of them would show up in
+these lines first.
+
+Run the same command with `--seconds` instead and the same lines come out, with `seconds=` beside
+them and a cycle count the machine decided. That is the difference between proving an instrument
+and reading a machine, and neither run is more honest than the other: one is the evidence a page
+may quote, and the other is the evidence only a sampler can read.
+
 ## Where the ideas come from
 
-<!-- TODO: the two or three standing ideas this page stands on — sampling versus counting, and the
-durability policy the write-path record argues for — and what this project takes from each. -->
+- **sampling versus counting.** A counter is instrumentation somebody wrote, so it reports the work
+  somebody thought to count. A sampler is an interruption nobody wrote: it reports the process as
+  it was, including the parts no counter covers. `perf stat` and `perf record` are those two halves
+  in the usual tooling, and the reason both exist is this page's finding.
+- **the durability policy.** The write-path record's argument — that waiting for the device is a
+  cost with a decided bound rather than a habit — is the change this profile justifies. Its
+  measured 4.173 ms is what makes the sampler's top line readable as a number rather than as a
+  symbol.
+- [`sample(1)`](https://ss64.com/mac/sample.html) — the platform sampler, its call graph and its
+  leaf-frame table. `man sample` on a Mac is the same page, and it is the tool the block above came
+  out of.
